 Abstract
Online platforms receive large volumes of comments, making manual review slow and inconsistent. This project presents an AI-powered text-analysis prototype that detects multiple forms of toxic language and returns a moderation recommendation. The deployed prototype uses a fine-tuned DistilBERT model, identified in the project files as Jigsaw 6-label Model V4, trained for multi-label classification on the Jigsaw Toxic Comment Classification Challenge dataset. The six model labels are toxic, severe_toxic, obscene, threat, insult, and identity_hate. A comment can activate more than one label.
The application uses Python, PyTorch, Hugging Face Transformers, FastAPI, Uvicorn, and an HTML/CSS/JavaScript interface. The inference pipeline tokenizes the input, produces six independent probabilities through sigmoid outputs, applies a threshold of 0.5, and derives user-facing prediction, score, severity, and moderation information. The backend exposes a /predict endpoint and the prototype has been deployed to Render. Project documentation reports approximate overall accuracy of 0.98, macro F1-score of 0.87, and toxic-label F1-score of 0.82. These figures are reported rather than independently recalculated here; the exact accuracy definition, per-label metrics, confusion matrix, and full evaluation outputs were not supplied. Therefore, the results should be interpreted cautiously and verified against saved evaluation artifacts before formal publication.
Keywords: Natural Language Processing; toxicity detection; cyberbullying; multi-label classification; DistilBERT; FastAPI; content moderation

 1. Introduction
Cyberbullying, harassment, hate-related language, insults, and threats can appear in social-media comments and online discussions. Human moderators cannot always inspect every comment quickly, while simple keyword filters may fail to recognize context or may flag harmless uses of words. Natural Language Processing (NLP) offers methods for examining text and identifying patterns associated with harmful content.
This project implements a web-based toxicity predictor. A user enters a comment, the frontend sends it to a FastAPI backend, and a fine-tuned DistilBERT model predicts six related labels. The application then presents a score and a suggested moderation outcome. The tool is intended to support human review, not replace it.
2. Problem Statement and Motivation
The problem addressed is the need for a scalable, consistent first-pass analysis of user comments. The system must classify text using contextual language representations, expose predictions through an API, and return understandable results. The motivation is to reduce repetitive manual screening and provide a prototype that can be demonstrated and tested through a web interface.
3. Objectives and Scope
•	Build a multi-label NLP classifier for six Jigsaw toxicity labels.
•	Use a pretrained Transformer model fine-tuned for toxicity detection.
•	Expose model inference through a FastAPI endpoint.
•	Display the prediction, confidence/score, severity and moderation recommendation.
•	Support local testing and a hosted prototype deployment.
•	Document limitations, evaluation evidence, and responsible-use considerations.
Scope note: the model’s native labels are the six Jigsaw labels. They are not identical to a separate six-class taxonomy such as Normal, Offensive, Toxic, Cyberbullying, Hate Speech, and Threat. In particular, the model is multi-label and may detect several labels simultaneously.
4. Related Work
Toxic-language detection has been studied using lexical rules, traditional machine-learning classifiers, and deep neural language models. Transformer encoders such as BERT-family models represent words in context and can be fine-tuned for text classification. DistilBERT is a compressed BERT-family model designed to reduce model size and inference cost while retaining much of the original model’s language capability. Public benchmark datasets, including the Jigsaw Toxic Comment Classification Challenge, provide comments with multiple binary toxicity annotations.
The present project focuses on a working prototype that combines a Transformer classifier with an API and a moderation layer. Its reported scores should not be compared directly with other papers unless dataset splits, label definitions, thresholds, and evaluation metrics match.
5. Dataset Description
5.1 Dataset 1: Cyberbully Detection Dataset
The earlier project dataset is named cb_multi_labeled_balanced.csv and uses text and label columns. The documented original labels are not_cyberbullying, ethnicity/race, gender/sexual, and religion. It supports the earlier cyberbullying-classification work but does not natively include every label in the deployed Jigsaw model.
5.2 Dataset 2: Jigsaw Toxic Comment Classification Challenge
The deployment model uses the Jigsaw training file with 159,571 comments and eight columns: comment_text plus six binary target columns and the record identifier. The six targets are toxic, severe_toxic, obscene, threat, insult, and identity_hate. A comment may have multiple positive labels, so multi-label learning is appropriate.
