5.3 Dataset Limitations
The Jigsaw labels are imbalanced; some labels, such as threat and identity_hate, are relatively rare. This can make overall accuracy misleading. The report’s available documentation does not provide exact post-cleaning label counts, the exact train/validation split, or complete per-label evaluation outputs. Those values should be computed from the actual training/evaluation files rather than estimated.
6. Proposed Methodology
The implemented inference workflow is: user input → frontend request → FastAPI validation → tokenizer → DistilBERT → six sigmoid probabilities → threshold-based label detection → score/severity and moderation logic → response displayed to the user. The training workflow uses labeled comments and six binary targets to fine-tune a pretrained Transformer model. The trained model is saved and loaded by the inference module.
 
Figure 1. End-to-end system workflow.
7. Text Preprocessing and Tokenization
The first project dataset’s documented preprocessing includes loading the CSV, removing duplicate records, lowercasing text, removing URLs and user mentions, and selecting the relevant text and label columns. For the Jigsaw deployment dataset, the exact extra cleaning steps are not fully specified in the available implementation notes. The tokenizer supplied with the selected DistilBERT model converts the comment into token IDs and attention masks, with truncation/padding according to the model configuration.
Preprocessing should preserve meaningful context. Removing all punctuation or emojis indiscriminately may remove signals relevant to tone. For a reproducible final report, the exact cleaning code, maximum sequence length, tokenizer checkpoint, split seed, and handling of missing records should be recorded from the final training script.
8. DistilBERT Model Architecture
DistilBERT is a Transformer encoder model derived from BERT through knowledge distillation. Its self-attention layers produce contextual representations, meaning a token’s representation can depend on surrounding words. A classification head maps the final representation to six logits, one for each Jigsaw label.
For each label, sigmoid converts the corresponding logit into a probability between zero and one. The prototype uses a threshold of 0.5: a label is marked as detected when its probability is at least 0.5. Unlike softmax multi-class classification, sigmoid outputs do not need to sum to one because multiple labels can be present in the same comment.
 
Figure 2. Multi-label output structure.
9. Multi-label Classification
This is a supervised multi-label classification project, not a regression project. Each training example contains a text input and six binary target values. The model independently estimates whether each label is present. The API may also display a derived toxicity score or confidence value, but these are application outputs based on model probabilities; they do not change the main task into regression.
