16. Responsible AI, Limitations and Future Work
16.1 Limitations
• Dataset label imbalance: Rare categories such as threat (<0.3%) and identity_hate (<0.9%) have lower precision due to class rarity.
• Context and nuance: Sarcasm, irony, in-group reclaimed language, and multi-turn conversations can result in false positives or false negatives.
• Fixed 0.5 threshold: A single threshold balances general recall, but validation-tuned thresholds (0.75-0.86) provide higher precision.
• Advisory role: Automated classifications must serve as assistive flags rather than autonomous final censors.
16.2 Future Improvements
• Expand multi-turn context awareness and conversational dialogue modeling.
• Implement human-in-the-loop review queues and user appeal mechanisms.
• Support multi-lingual toxicity analysis across diverse cultural dialects.
17. Conclusion
The AI-Powered Cyberbullying / Toxicity Predictor demonstrates an end-to-end prototype for multi-label toxic-language detection using a fine-tuned DistilBERT model, a FastAPI backend, and an interactive web interface. Independent test evaluation confirms 0.9794 element-wise Hamming accuracy, 0.9087 subset exact-match accuracy, 0.8099 toxic F1-score, and 0.9903 mean ROC-AUC across 15,958 held-out comments. The prototype demonstrates fast local inference (mean 9.28 ms) and contextual moderation routing.
